                <pb facs="0360" n="266" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg266"/>

                <head>No. 215.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Dinsmore</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-05-11"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 11,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 11.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received Mr. Rockhill’s dispatch No.
                    72, of March 31 last, reporting the accidental killing of William McKay, an
                    American citizen, by a Corean on the 8th of that month, at Seoul.</p>
                <p>Although interference with the course of municipal law, even though it be to
                    solicit clemency for the author of an injury to a foreigner, is delicate and
                    exceptional, yet, under the circumstances and in view of the wide gulf which
                    separates Corean judicial methods from our own, the appeal of Mr. Rockhill,
                    responding as it did to the widow’s earnest solicitation is most
                    commendable.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
